This page is the title page and opening of Riemann's student paper 'Versuch einer allgemeinen Auffassung der Integration und Differentiation' (dated January 1847, published posthumously as No. XIX in the Gesammelte Werke, 1876). It contains no derivation, only the programme.

In modern terms: for functions f of one variable x, Riemann seeks a family of operators D^ν indexed by a number ν. The value g = D^ν f should depend on f in a way 'expressed by a number', and must satisfy three consistency conditions:
- for ν = n a positive integer, D^n f = d^n f/dx^n;
- for ν = -n a negative integer, D^{-n} f is the n-fold integral of f;
- for ν = 0, D^0 f = f.

He then states his methodological stance. Ordinary differential and integral calculus is assumed as foundation. However, the laws known to hold for integer orders, such as the index law D^a D^b = D^{a+b}, are not simply to be extended by fiat to fractional orders. Instead, the integer-order results are to be used for two purposes:
- to justify the procedure;
- as a 'guide' (Wegweiser) to finding it.

The continuation on the next page makes the plan concrete. Starting from the Taylor expansion of f(x+h) in powers of h, he will interpolate the sequence of derivatives to non-integer indices. This is the route that leads, later in the paper, to the Riemann–Liouville integral (1/Γ(ν)) ∫_k^x (x-t)^{ν-1} f(t) dt, including a 'complementary function' for the undetermined lower limit.
